WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.402
-Our next guest
is a talented actress

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:03.903
you know from her work
on "Stranger Things"

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.771
and Taylor Swift's
"All Too Well."

00:00:05.771 --> 00:00:08.308
She stars in "The Whale,"
which is in theaters now.

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:09.308
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.377
-You can just be angry at me.

00:00:11.377 --> 00:00:13.078
-Okay.
You know what?

00:00:13.078 --> 00:00:15.448
You can't throw me away
like I'm a piece of garbage

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:18.651
and then suddenly just want
to be my dad eight years later.

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:21.721
You left me for your boyfriend.

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:23.323
It's that simple.

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:25.224
And if you've been telling
yourself anything different,

00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:27.227
then you're lying to yourself.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.332
But you know what?
I'm glad. I'm --

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:35.801
I'm glad, because you taught me
something very important.

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:39.706
People are ass[bleep]

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:42.475
Most people learn that
way too late.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:45.177
You -- You taught me that
when I was 8.

00:00:45.177 --> 00:00:48.314
-Please welcome to the show
Sadie Sink, everybody!

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:50.183
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:57.089 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.089 --> 00:01:03.997 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:05.664
Hi!
I'm so happy you're here.

00:01:05.664 --> 00:01:07.066
-Thank you for having me.

00:01:07.066 --> 00:01:08.734
-Congratulations on this film.

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:12.137
It's receiving tons of accolades
and a lot of nominations.

00:01:12.137 --> 00:01:15.808
It also seems like
such an intensely hard film

00:01:15.808 --> 00:01:17.343
to be an actor in.

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:19.211
Was it brutal to sort of
find connection

00:01:19.211 --> 00:01:21.681
to somebody who is so hurt
and so angry?

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:23.850
-I mean, yeah, I guess at first.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.585
We had a three-week
rehearsal period, though.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:27.687
So we kind of all took that time

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:31.057
to just really dig deeper
into these characters.

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:33.325
And I don't know.
They're very complex.

00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:34.427
-They're very complex.

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.262
-Just to find the anger
was important.

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.698
-Brendan Fraser is very complex
in this film

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:41.367
but in real life is one of the
sweetest people in the world.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:45.371
Were you familiar with Brendan
outside this film

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:46.572
before you started
working on it?

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:47.840
-No, I wasn't.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.109
I hadn't seen a single one
of his films.

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.944
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:55.314
I know, but I just knew him
as this character,

00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:57.417
which was pretty cool,
but then afterwards, I was like,

00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:00.253
"Okay, I have to watch
one of his movies."

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:01.621
So that's been really crazy,

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:04.023
'cause I had no idea
he was a very comedic actor.

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:06.025
It was shocking.
-Yeah, no.

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:08.561
It's crazy to think that when
they were casting this movie,

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:10.629
they thought, "We should get
George of the Jungle."

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:12.198
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.232
But it works.
-But he is.

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:15.301
He is the funniest,
sweetest guy.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:18.003
-Mm-hmm.
-So you got your start

00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:21.373
as a very young actor
on Broadway.

00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:22.475
You were Annie.

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:24.644
How old when you
were doing "Annie"?

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:25.911
-10, I think.

00:02:25.911 --> 00:02:27.046
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:28.948
-There you are as Annie.
-Yes, there's Annie.

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:32.485
-I think that's it right there,
yeah.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:33.886
And this is pretty cool.

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:36.189
You were in "The Audience"
with Helen Mirren.

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:37.657
You played a young Helen Mirren.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:38.858
-Yes. Yeah.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.293
-That's fantastic.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:42.395
-So how old were you
in this one?

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:44.897
-12, I think,
just turned 13 or something.

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:48.701
-So you do these huge things,
and then when it's over,

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.870
you just go back to being --

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.838
-A kid, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:52.838 --> 00:02:55.041
How is it to go from Broadway

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:58.010
and I'm sure what is
a real adrenaline rush

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.912
and then going back
to just being a kid?

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:02.014
-Honestly, I was more nervous
to, like, go to school

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:04.517
than I was to be onstage.

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.819
But, yeah,
after I finished that show,

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:08.587
I had nothing else lined up.

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:11.123
So I was like, "Okay,
it's time to go back to school."

00:03:11.123 --> 00:03:14.093
And I was homeschooled
from, like, ages 9 to 13.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:17.096
And, like, the curriculum
was just very loose.

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:19.832
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:22.769
-This is sort of the Broadway --
necessity of Broadway.

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:26.472
-It was like belting 101 and,
like, how to do a time step.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:27.907
Like, that was pretty much it.

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:30.743
But then, yeah, when I was 13,
it was time to go to school.

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:34.547
And, yeah -- And so I did it.
-You did it.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.649
And ultimately,
it's so funny to think, like --

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:38.618
Going to high school, it's like,

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:41.787
"Look, it's a lot harder
than Broadway."

00:03:41.787 --> 00:03:43.590
-Yeah.
Honestly, yeah.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.826
I think it was nice to just be
around kids my age, though.

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:47.793
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:49.062
-Now, it is true that --

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.431
Speaking about being
around kids your age,

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:54.566
is it true that the Broadway
kids that are sort of younger,

00:03:54.566 --> 00:03:57.170
there's one playground you all
would go and hang out at?

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:58.471
-Yes.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:00.740
-So when would you go
to the Broadway playground?

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.175
And was everybody -- was it like
♪ The swings, the swings ♪

00:04:03.175 --> 00:04:04.544
-Yes. Yes.

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:09.248
It was like on two-show days,
everyone would just go.

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:11.383
All the moms would just bring
their kids to this park

00:04:11.383 --> 00:04:14.119
on, like, 43rd and 8th, I think.
-Okay.

00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:15.855
-But it didn't look like
a normal playground.

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:18.457
There was always some kind
of musical number happening.

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:21.126
Or, like, they were teaching
the choreography to each other,

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:22.228
and there was cliques.

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:23.529
Like, the "Matilda" kids
were always, like,

00:04:23.529 --> 00:04:26.965
much cooler than everybody else.

00:04:26.965 --> 00:04:29.334
Yeah, and then, like,
if a kid would start screaming

00:04:29.334 --> 00:04:31.637
or something, like, yelling and
playing, a mom would be like,

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:36.142
"No, honey, rest your voice.
You have a show at 8:00."

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.610
-Now, your parents
are educators.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.779
Both of them.
-Yes. Yes.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:40.747
-Was that helpful?

00:04:40.747 --> 00:04:42.114
I mean, again, you obviously

00:04:42.114 --> 00:04:44.016
had a very unconventional
upbringing.

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.286
But it must have been helpful to
have parents who were teachers.

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:48.421
-Oh, totally.
My dad is a math teacher.

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.857
And that really came in handy.

00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:53.059
When I was 13
going back to school,

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:55.828
I had to take
a math placement test.

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:59.399
So then my dad, like, sat me
down in one week, and he's like,

00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:02.501
"Alright, I'm going to teach you
everything you need to know."

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:04.670
And then I went in on Monday
and I tested average.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:07.006
-Oh, wow.
-Yes.

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:09.575
I did it.
[ Applause ]

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:13.046
-Yeah, for a normal kid,
you're average at math.

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:14.914
For a Broadway kid, exceptional.

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:16.449
-Yes.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:20.419
-Now, "Stranger Things," I have
to ask about "Stranger Things."

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:22.121
It must be so cool to be
in a show like that

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:23.455
that so many people watch.

00:05:23.455 --> 00:05:26.225
But I'm wondering, do you
feel the pressure with that

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:28.828
of friends and family
who are desperate

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:31.163
to know what's going to happen
in the show before it airs?

00:05:31.163 --> 00:05:32.498
-Oh, yeah, I mean, I'm bad

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:35.034
and I'll just give my family
the scripts.

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:36.201
-Oh, wow.
-Yeah.

00:05:36.201 --> 00:05:38.003
-So you don't play by the

00:05:38.003 --> 00:05:39.705
"everything needs
to be embargoed."

00:05:39.705 --> 00:05:41.907
-Just, you know,
like, my brothers,

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:44.076
if they want to read it,
I'll let them.

00:05:44.076 --> 00:05:47.279
I think that's okay.
-Yeah.

00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:48.580
-They don't tell anyone,

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.817
but, yeah, they're big fans
of the show.

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:51.817
-It's funny.

00:05:51.817 --> 00:05:53.419
Would you want to know
in advance

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:54.887
if you were a fan
of a show they were in,

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:56.856
or would you want to wait
until you saw it on-screen?

00:05:56.856 --> 00:06:00.293
-I mean, if you have access
to the scripts...

00:06:00.293 --> 00:06:01.793
-You want to get your hands
on the script.

00:06:01.793 --> 00:06:03.162
Well, thank you
for your honesty.

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:04.997
I really appreciate it.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:04.997 --> 00:06:07.133
You -- There's five of you,
right, siblings?

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:08.334
-Yeah.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.002
-And so where are you
on the pecking order?

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:11.804
-I'm number four.
-Okay, got you.

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:12.805
-And I have a little sister.

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:14.507
-And your little sister

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:17.810
played a younger version of you
in "The Whale."

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.012
-Yes.
-So how did this come about?

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:21.547
Here you are with her.
-Uh-huh.

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:24.116
-I mean, I guess it seemed like
natural casting when they --

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:26.819
-I mean, she's a mini me.
But --

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:29.655
-Did they ask you
before they had casting?

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:31.523
Were they like, "Do you have
a smaller one of you at home?"

00:06:31.523 --> 00:06:33.859
-Yes.
I was like, "Yes, yes, I do.

00:06:33.859 --> 00:06:36.329
What time do you need us?"

00:06:36.329 --> 00:06:38.331
Yeah, so Darren had added --
the director,

00:06:38.331 --> 00:06:42.735
he added this flashback sequence
where they're at a beach

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:44.903
and they needed
an 8-year-old version of me.

00:06:44.903 --> 00:06:46.672
So they asked for Jacey.

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:48.140
And then, yeah --
and then we did it.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:49.876
And she still hasn't
seen it yet.

00:06:49.876 --> 00:06:52.745
-Got you. Did Jacey,
sort of negotiation-wise,

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:54.680
hold them over a barrel
'cause she had them?

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.281
Was she like,
"Now that you've asked,

00:06:56.281 --> 00:06:59.318
I'm the only one
that looks like her"?

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:01.086
-No, she had a lot of fun.

00:07:01.086 --> 00:07:02.488
She was little petty

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:04.857
because my entire family
went to the premiere

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:08.160
and it's, like, too mature
for her, so she stayed at home.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.396
And she's like,
"Have fun at my movie, guys.

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:11.597
Thanks a lot."

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.599
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:16.168
-That is -- I mean, what I
wouldn't have given as a kid

00:07:16.168 --> 00:07:18.337
to be like,
"Have fun at my premiere."

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:20.039
-Uh-huh.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:21.941
-You were also in --

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:23.909
I will admit that I did not --

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:25.644
I certainly did not remember
that this was you.

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:28.581
"The Americans" is a show
I loved a great deal.

00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:31.851
Was this your first on-screen?

00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:34.119
-Yes. That was my first time
on a film.

00:07:34.119 --> 00:07:36.155
-And there you are
with a shotgun.

00:07:36.155 --> 00:07:39.525
-So scared.
I have no clue what I'm doing.

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.026
-So -- [ Laughs ]

00:07:41.026 --> 00:07:43.762
And I'm assuming based on
the age you are in this photo,

00:07:43.762 --> 00:07:45.865
you probably weren't a huge fan
of "The Americans."

00:07:45.865 --> 00:07:50.402
-No, no, I honestly had no idea
where I was or what I was doing.

00:07:50.402 --> 00:07:52.672
And then I didn't know
what the term "rolling" meant.

00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:55.174
Nobody told me.
So I was kind of clueless.

00:07:55.174 --> 00:07:56.975
But yeah, that was my debut.

00:07:56.975 --> 00:07:58.878
-That breaks my heart that
you were kind of clueless.

00:07:58.878 --> 00:08:00.379
They were just like,
"Give that kid a gun."

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:02.815
-Exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.815 --> 00:08:03.916
-"We want her to look scared.

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:05.351
So let's tell her
as little as possible.

00:08:05.351 --> 00:08:07.620
Just put the gun in her hand."

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.289
I would imagine working on
something like "Stranger Things"

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:13.559
and working on "The Whale" very
different experiences on set.

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:16.261
Especially just because, again,
on "Stranger Things,"

00:08:16.261 --> 00:08:18.263
you are surrounded
by other actors your age.

00:08:18.263 --> 00:08:19.832
-Oh, totally,
other actors my age,

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:22.768
but then also, like,
men in green suits

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:24.937
and tennis balls on sticks
and stuff.

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:28.273
So you get really used to
working with that.

00:08:28.273 --> 00:08:31.744
-Yeah, I feel like
not enough credit is given

00:08:31.744 --> 00:08:34.213
to people who act
with tennis balls on sticks.

00:08:34.213 --> 00:08:36.082
-It's hard.
-It really is hard.

00:08:36.082 --> 00:08:38.251
I was once on set of a movie

00:08:38.251 --> 00:08:40.920
where a very famous actor
was doing a scene

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.388
with a tennis ball and a stick
that would later be a monster.

00:08:43.388 --> 00:08:45.024
And I was just watching it
and I was thinking,

00:08:45.024 --> 00:08:46.893
"I shouldn't be here,
because this is --"

00:08:46.893 --> 00:08:49.361
-You feel ridiculous.
-Of course, yeah.

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:52.064
-What am I -- What am I doing?
What are we doing?

00:08:52.064 --> 00:08:54.633
But it looks good in the end,
I guess.

00:08:54.633 --> 00:08:55.968
[ Laughter ]

00:08:55.968 --> 00:08:57.136
-It does look good in the end.

00:08:57.136 --> 00:08:58.604
You don't have to guess.
It looks good in the end.

00:08:58.604 --> 00:08:59.905
But I want you to know,

00:08:59.905 --> 00:09:01.707
'cause when I watch a show
like "Stranger Things,"

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:02.908
I'm even more impressed now

00:09:02.908 --> 00:09:05.177
because I know
these creatures aren't there.

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:07.412
That is like a Wilson
tennis ball on a stick

00:09:07.412 --> 00:09:08.914
and there's a dude,
like, doing this.

00:09:08.914 --> 00:09:10.316
-Yeah. Exactly, exactly.

00:09:10.316 --> 00:09:11.817
-And I just have
a great appreciation

00:09:11.817 --> 00:09:12.918
for what you're all doing.

00:09:12.918 --> 00:09:15.154
-Oh, thank you.

00:09:15.154 --> 00:09:20.225
-Tell me, also, you, of course,
were in the Taylor Swift video.

00:09:20.225 --> 00:09:22.294
It feels more
like a video than --

00:09:22.294 --> 00:09:25.597
Calling it a video
seems a little reductive.

00:09:25.597 --> 00:09:26.966
What was that like?

00:09:26.966 --> 00:09:29.769
Was that a situation
where you auditioned,

00:09:29.769 --> 00:09:30.902
or did you get a phone call?

00:09:30.902 --> 00:09:32.071
How did it come to be?

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:34.339
-Yeah, I mean,
I was a fan of hers before.

00:09:34.339 --> 00:09:35.408
And then her --

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:37.910
-Brendan Fraser
should be so lucky.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:40.512
[ Laughter ]

00:09:40.512 --> 00:09:42.014
-That would be great
if you were like,

00:09:42.014 --> 00:09:44.650
"I had never heard of her,
much like Brendan Fraser,

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.251
but I went back
and I listened to her songs,

00:09:46.251 --> 00:09:47.586
and she is very good."
-I'm like, "Wow, yeah,

00:09:47.586 --> 00:09:49.121
she's good."

00:09:49.121 --> 00:09:51.390
But I did know Taylor Swift.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.492
And then her team reached out,

00:09:53.492 --> 00:09:55.995
and I guess she had me in mind
for this video.

00:09:55.995 --> 00:09:58.230
So, you know, I said yes,
of course.

00:09:58.230 --> 00:09:59.298
And yeah.

00:09:59.298 --> 00:10:01.400
-Was that --
I mean, of all the things

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.835
that you have been able
to tell people, oh, you know,

00:10:03.835 --> 00:10:05.837
your friends or your family,
"Hey, I'm about to do this,

00:10:05.837 --> 00:10:08.574
I'm about to do that,"
was that the trippiest?

00:10:08.574 --> 00:10:10.809
-Yes, because
you would never think

00:10:10.809 --> 00:10:12.912
that our paths
would really cross,

00:10:12.912 --> 00:10:14.313
like, someone being
in the music industry

00:10:14.313 --> 00:10:15.781
and then in the film industry.

00:10:15.781 --> 00:10:19.184
Like, it just never --
two different worlds.

00:10:19.184 --> 00:10:21.854
So it was kind of like
a bizarre mash-up.

00:10:21.854 --> 00:10:24.023
But everyone was very excited.

00:10:24.023 --> 00:10:25.858
-Well, she chose very well,

00:10:25.858 --> 00:10:28.426
as has everybody that has been
lucky enough to work with you.

00:10:28.426 --> 00:10:29.695
It is so lovely
to have you here.

00:10:29.695 --> 00:10:31.363
Thanks so much
for making the time.

00:10:31.363 --> 00:10:33.231
Sadie Sink, everybody.

00:10:33.231 --> 00:10:35.000
"The Whale" is in theaters now.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.769
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

